<p>
<font face="arial"><b>A MONTH OF MONITORING</b></font><br>
<i>Wildlife Habitat Institute (WHI), Pocket Water, Inc. (PWI), and University of Idaho Eco-Hydraulics Research Group (UI)</i>
<p>
Jenn Golden (PWI), Dave Fuhrman (UI), Gloria Beattie (UI), Steve Clayton (UI), and Kurt Dostal (WHI) spent a large part of August collecting monitoring data.  The majority of these activities occurred during the week of August 10th - 14th and are listed below:
<ul>
<li>Dave collected discharge, temperature, and groundwater measurements.  
<li>Jenn and Gloria collected the established 12 photopoints and two panoramic views.
<li>Jenn and Dave collected data on stream substrate distribution and percent surface fines using the pebble count and wire grid methods.  At each station, replicate samples were collected to provide an estimate of method precision.  The four stations in Phase I (1996) construction area were re-sampled and two new stations were established in Phase II (1997) construction area.  Two additional stations were located in the Phase III (1999) area to assist in evaluating the shift in substrate composition due to the past two years of construction.  These data will provide useful information to the design team regarding sediment movement and deposition in the constructed reaches.
<li>Dave and Jenn's attempt to download the turbidity/surface water elevation datalogger failed when an error in the laptop computer occurred.  The turbidity and water level gauges, however, are working.  The information has been not lost but will be collected in September.
<li>Kurt, Jenn, and Steve C. established five new greenline/riparian composition transects in Phase II (1997) construction area.  Data were collected from these transects as well as from the eight transects previously established in Phase I (1996) construction area.  All data were sent to Jenn for analyses and reporting.
<li>Kurt established transect sites and collected data to evaluate the revegetation success of this field season's plantings.  These transects will be re-surveyed in the summer of 1999 to determine survival rate of plants in relation to planting methods, seasonal weather patterns, and wildlife browsing.
<img align="right" src="kurt_grass.jpg" width=185 height=250 alt="" border="1">  
<li>Kurt collected data to determine growth differences between woody plants inside 	versus outside of the exclosures.  The exclosures were built for two primary purposes.  	One was to describe or quantify wildlife grazing pressure on newly planted woody 	vegetation.  The second was to provide an on-site seed source for woody vegetation 	should plants outside the exclosures be decimated by wildlife grazing.  Complete 	growth of each plant within the exclosures was measured and will be compared 	to a sample of plants outside.  Although these data have not yet been formally 	analyzed, differences are obvious by casual observation:  the plants inside the 	exclosures are larger and more vigorous than those outside.  As the project continues 	to add more willows each year, we hope wildlife will eventually become overwhelmed and differences in growth rates will become less noticeable.  
<li>Steve C., Gloria, and Dave located and resurveyed cross section pins in Phase I and Phase II construction areas and in the future Phase III area.  These pins were located to facilitate cross section surveys in Fall 1998 and to assess the accuracy of the CE Engineering and UI survey class surveys from 1997.  Steve C. and Gloria also labeled all cross section pins in Phase III.  All monitoring well locations, the discharge station, the "skipped" wetland data point, building and road locations, and miscellaneous points of interest were also surveyed.  We are investigating the best method for relocating pins for future vegetation and cross section monitoring that also protects the aesthetic beauty of the meadow and prevents safety hazards.  A variety of GPS systems have been recommended, but none has been field-tested.  
</ul>

